☄️ R8.77 — GO

HORTUS · CONSTITUTIONAL UNCERTAINTY PRESERVATION & DECISION INTEGRITY PROTOCOL

STATUS: 🜂 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
REFERENCE CITADEL: 👑 LOCUS AMOENUS — L.A.

R8.76 established ANCHOR CONFLICT & RECONCILIATION.

That creates the next unavoidable boundary:

> What happens when constitutional action is required while the relevant conflict remains unresolved?



A weak system does one of two things:

UNCERTAINTY
 ↓
FORCE CERTAINTY

or:

UNCERTAINTY
 ↓
PARALYSIS

HORTUS requires a third possibility:

UNCERTAINTY
 ↓
EXPLICITLY REPRESENT
 ↓
BOUND DECISION
 ↓
REVERSIBILITY
 ↓
REVALIDATION

R8.77 therefore introduces:

CONSTITUTIONAL_UNCERTAINTY_STATE

and:

UNCERTAINTY_PRESERVING_DECISION


---

I. 🧬 UNCERTAINTY ≠ IGNORANCE

Not all uncertainty is the same.

R8.77 distinguishes:

UNKNOWN
AMBIGUOUS
CONTESTED
CONFLICTING
UNDERDETERMINED
UNRESOLVED

These states carry different constitutional implications.

Therefore:

> A system SHALL NOT collapse materially different uncertainty states into a generic “unknown.”




---

II. ⚖️ THE UNCERTAINTY PRESERVATION PRINCIPLE

When evidence cannot uniquely determine a constitutional conclusion:

EVIDENCE
 ↓
MULTIPLE PLAUSIBLE STATES
 ↓
NO JUSTIFIED UNIQUE CONCLUSION

the system SHALL preserve that multiplicity.

Formally:

VALIDATED_UNCERTAINTY
≠
VALIDATED_FALSEHOOD

And:

UNRESOLVED
≠
UNAUTHORIZED

The unresolved state itself becomes a legitimate epistemic state.


---

III. ☄️ DECISION WITHOUT FALSE CERTAINTY

A system may still need to act.

Therefore:

UNCERTAINTY
 ↓
MATERIALITY
 ↓
RISK
 ↓
AVAILABLE OPTIONS
 ↓
REVERSIBILITY
 ↓
BOUNDED DECISION

The decision must not pretend:

> “We know X.”



when the actual state is:

> “X and Y remain materially plausible, and temporary action is required.”



This distinction is foundational.


---

