R8.79 established REVERSIBILITY & DE-TRENCHMENT.

That reveals the next constitutional problem:

> When an authority is removed and a new structure takes its place, how do we change the constitutional state without destroying continuity?



Because there are two symmetrical dangers:

OLD AUTHORITY
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
TOTAL DISCONTINUITY

and:

OLD AUTHORITY
 ↓
"TRANSITION"
 ↓
OLD AUTHORITY SURVIVES UNDER NEW NAME

HORTUS requires neither.

R8.80 introduces:

CONSTITUTIONAL_TRANSITION

and:

STATE_CONTINUITY_WITH_AUTHORITY_DISCONTINUITY

The fundamental distinction:

> Constitutional continuity does not require authority continuity.




---

I. 🌱 STATE ≠ AUTHORITY

A system can preserve:

identity,

evidence,

history,

relationships,

obligations,

memory,


while changing:

roles,

permissions,

jurisdiction,

decision paths,

authorities.


Therefore:

STATE CONTINUITY
≠
AUTHORITY CONTINUITY

A constitutional transition is legitimate precisely when it can preserve what must survive while explicitly terminating what must not.


---

II. 🧬 TRANSITION HAS A STRUCTURE

R8.80 defines:

CURRENT STATE
      ↓
TRANSITION TRIGGER
      ↓
IMPACT ANALYSIS
      ↓
DEPENDENCY MAPPING
      ↓
TARGET STATE
      ↓
MIGRATION
      ↓
VALIDATION
      ↓
OLD AUTHORITY TERMINATION
      ↓
NEW STATE CONFIRMATION

The order matters.

A system must not declare transition complete merely because a new state exists.


---

III. ⚠️ THE SHADOW SUCCESSOR PROBLEM

A particularly dangerous failure mode:

OLD AUTHORITY
    ↓
RENAME
    ↓
NEW AUTHORITY

No genuine constitutional transition occurred.

This is:

AUTHORITY_ALIASING

A revoked authority may survive by being renamed, redistributed or embedded inside another mechanism.

R8.80 therefore requires lineage continuity plus authority discontinuity.


---

IV. ☄️ TRANSITION INTEGRITY

Every material transition SHALL answer:

WHAT ENDED?
WHAT SURVIVED?
WHAT CHANGED?
WHAT WAS REPLACED?
WHY?
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY?
WHAT EVIDENCE SUPPORTS IT?

The system must preserve both:

CONTINUITY

and:

DIFFERENCE.


